/-!
# Hilbert decomposition over a rational place of K(t)

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.ord_restrictAlong_eq_natCard_algHom_of_isGalois`
(`8e50de98-6fdc-590e-b2ee-1ad607fe9b17`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `3200129a-66ed-582e-b880-3c9441cb337b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $F$, $M$ be fields equipped with $K$-algebra
structures. Fix $x \in F$, $t \in M$, $j_0 \in K$, a $K$-algebra homomorphism $\iota : F \to M$ with
$\iota(x) = t$, and assume $M$ is a finite-dimensional Galois extension of the subfield $K\langle
t\rangle$ generated by $t$ over $K$, and that for every $K$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : F \to M$
with $\psi(x) = t$ the underlying ring homomorphism is integral. Let $W_0$ be a place of $M$ over
$K$ (a valuation subring of $M$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from $M$, whose ring is a
principal ideal ring) with $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0}(t - j_0) > 0$, the order being minus the
logarithm of the associated adic valuation. Writing $D$ for the set of $\sigma \in
\mathrm{Gal}(M/K\langle t\rangle)$ whose associated semilinear automorphism $(\sigma,
\mathrm{id}_K)$ fixes $W_0$, and $W_0|_\psi$ for the place of $F$ with valuation subring
$\psi^{-1}(\mathcal O_{W_0})$, the conclusion is the conjunction: (1) $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0}(t -
j_0) = \#D$; (2) $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0|_\psi}(x - j_0) > 0$ for every such $\psi$; (3) every
place $w$ of $F$ over $K$ with $\operatorname{ord}_w(x - j_0) > 0$ equals $W_0|_\psi$ for some such
$\psi$; (4) $W_0|_\psi = W_0|_{\psi'}$ if and only if $\psi' = \sigma \circ \psi$ for some $\sigma
\in D$; (5) $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0|_\psi}(x - j_0)$ equals the number of $\psi'$ with $\psi'(x) =
t$ and $W_0|_{\psi'} = W_0|_\psi$.

This is Hilbert's decomposition theory for a place of an intermediate field of a Galois extension,
specialised to a place of $M$ above the rational place $t = j_0$ of $K(t)$ with $K$ algebraically
closed, so that residue degrees are $1$ and ramification indices are orders of decomposition groups;
the places of $F$ above $x = j_0$ are thereby identified with the classes of $K$-embeddings $F \to
M$ sending $x$ to $t$ modulo the decomposition group. It underlies the double-coset count of places
and the ramification contributions used in the degree and genus estimates for modular curves.

